  "id": "shaoju_shaoxing_luantan",
  "name": "Shaoju (Shaoxing luantan opera)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Shaoxing luantan opera of eastern Zhejiang, sung in Shaoxing dialect, renamed Shaoju in the 1950s and best known for its Monkey King plays, one of which was filmed in 1960; it entered China's national intangible heritage list in 2006. Its erfan tunes are led by the banhu fiddle with the douzi supporting it, while the sanwuqi, yanglu and xilu tunes are led by bamboo flute; the traditional six-man band of drum-and-clappers, two wind players, sanxian, cymbals and small gong, plus a big gong, was enlarged with sheng, pipa and zhonghu from the mid-1950s.",
  "recipe": "classic 1954-1956 shaoju, art Music, east Asian branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. gutty bowl resonator coconut shell paulownia banhu, bamboo credited bamboo flute unspecified, sanxian, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, naobo, opera bending gong tamtam. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
